<description>
Extend each colored marker through its full row and column, marking crossings between different markers red.

Input structure:
- The grid has a dominant background color and two or more isolated non-background marker pixels.
- Marker pixels have distinct rows, distinct columns, and distinct non-red colors.

Rule:
- For each marker, paint its entire row and entire column with the marker color.
- Where the row or column from one marker crosses the row or column from another marker, paint red.
- Preserve the original marker pixels in their own colors.

Key insight:
- The sparse marker pixels are anchors for full-grid horizontal and vertical guide lines; only cross-marker intersections get the special red color.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "each isolated non-background marker pixel s",
  "path": "four straight rays from s along its row and column to the grid edges",
  "trajectory": "paint every cell on s.row and s.column with s.color",
  "transform": "paint crossings between paths from different markers red; keep original marker pixels in their own colors"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 13-16 rows x 13-16 cols, square (H == W), background black
  markers:
    count: 2-3 isolated single-pixel non-background cells
    colors: each marker has a distinct non-red color; red (2) is reserved for crossings
    positions: all rows distinct, all columns distinct; each >=1 cell from any edge [observed]
    spacing: row gaps >=2, col gaps >=2 between any pair [observed]
  output_rule: |
    For each marker, paint its entire row and column with marker color.
    At every crossing of row_i x col_j where i != j, paint red (2).
    Original marker pixels keep their own color (overriding red).
    Red crossing count = n*(n-1) for n markers.

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input is simple; no complex constraints to invert)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size S in [13..16]; create SxS black grid.
    2. Pick n in {2, 3} markers.
    3. Choose n distinct non-red colors from {1,3,4,5,6,7,8,9}.
    4. Choose n distinct rows and n distinct columns, each in [1..S-2] with pairwise gaps >=2.
    5. Place one marker pixel at each (row_i, col_i) with its color.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

